 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{tri} \cdot N_{vox\_per\_tri})$, where $N_{tri}$ is the number of triangles and $N_{vox\_per\_tri}$ is the number of voxels in the triangle's bounding box. In the worst case, $O(N_{tri} \cdot N^3)$ if triangles span the entire grid, but typically much less due to bounding box culling [S1, S2].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^3 / 32)$ for the voxel table, plus $O(N_{tri})$ for triangle data [S1].
-- **Performance:** Real-time performance (~60 FPS) at high voxel densities (~1M voxels) on modern GPUs [S2]. Exact results, but computationally expensive for high triangle counts due to per-voxel-triangle intersection tests [S2, S3].
-- **Strengths:** Exact geometric tests, parallelizable, no rasterization artifacts [S1, S2].
-- **Limitations:** Memory intensive for high resolutions due to dense bit grid (unless sparse extraction is used immediately), slow for very high triangle counts [S2].
-- **When to use:** When exact surface occupancy is required and the mesh is not excessively large [S2].
-- **When not to use:** For extremely high triangle counts without spatial acceleration, as it performs brute-force triangle-by-voxel testing [S2, S3].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{tri} \cdot N_{vox\_per\_tri})$, where $N_{tri}$ is the number of triangles and $N_{vox\_per\_tri}$ is the number of voxels in the triangle's bounding box [S2, unofficial]. In the worst case, $O(N_{tri} \cdot N^3)$ if triangles span the entire grid, but typically much less due to bounding box culling [S1, unofficial, S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^3 / 32)$ for the voxel table, plus $O(N_{tri})$ for triangle data [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Real-time performance (~60 FPS) at high voxel densities (~1M voxels) on modern GPUs [S2, unofficial]. Exact results, but computationally expensive for high triangle counts due to per-voxel-triangle intersection tests [S2, unofficial, S3, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Exact geometric tests, parallelizable, no rasterization artifacts [S1, unofficial, S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Memory intensive for high resolutions due to dense bit grid (unless sparse extraction is used immediately), slow for very high triangle counts [S2, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** When exact surface occupancy is required and the mesh is not excessively large [S2, unofficial].
+- **When not to use:** For extremely high triangle counts without spatial acceleration, as it performs brute-force triangle-by-voxel testing [S2, unofficial, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originated from the paper "Fast parallel surface and solid voxelization on GPUs" by Schwarz & Seidel (2010) [S3]. The algorithm was implemented in CUDA/C++ and demonstrated on NVIDIA GPUs [S3]. It is used in real-time applications requiring mesh-to-voxel conversion, such as gameplay systems and rendering [S2].
+Originated from the paper "Fast parallel surface and solid voxelization on GPUs" by Schwarz & Seidel (2010) [S3, unofficial]. The algorithm was implemented in CUDA/C++ and demonstrated on NVIDIA GPUs [S3, unofficial]. It is used in real-time applications requiring mesh-to-voxel conversion, such as gameplay systems and rendering [S2, unofficial].
 
